    {"id":"10bedf06-cc77-487a-80e3-60e0a7a2e729","term":"Transfer Function measurement","verdict":"MINOR","suggestions":[{"field":"definition","severity":"Medium","issue":"The definition says the technique captures frequency and phase response '(using swept tones or impulse responses)', but the entry's own purpose_function, practical_application, and scenario_contexts all describe the dual-channel FFT method (dual-channel analyzer, coherence, Smaart, delay finder). Web-verified via Rational Acoustics (support.rationalacoustics.com, Measurement 101) and Rane/Bob McCarthy material: a transfer function measurement is fundamentally a dual-channel FFT comparison of a reference (input) signal to a measured (output) signal, and the most common stimulus is random/pseudorandom (pink) noise or the actual program signal — not specifically swept tones or impulse responses. The method clause is therefore internally inconsistent with the rest of the entry and mischaracterizes the standard live-sound technique.","suggestion":"Revise the parenthetical to name the actual method, e.g. 'using a dual-channel FFT analyzer that compares a reference (input) signal to the measured (output) signal — driven by broadband noise or program material, and equally derivable from a swept-sine/impulse-response measurement.'"}]},
